WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.720
One way to learn the parameters
of the neural network,

2
00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:06.225
so that it gives
you a good encoding

3
00:00:06.225 --> 00:00:07.620
for your pictures of faces,

4
00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.150
is to define and apply

5
00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:11.940
gradient descent on the
triplet loss function.

6
00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.425
Let's see what that means.

7
00:00:13.425 --> 00:00:15.780
To apply the triplet loss you

8
00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.645
need to compare pairs of images.

9
00:00:18.645 --> 00:00:21.390
For example, given this picture,

10
00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:24.105
to learn the parameters
of the neural network,

11
00:00:24.105 --> 00:00:27.300
you have to look at several
pictures at the same time.

12
00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:29.700
For example, given
this pair of images,

13
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.200
you want their encodings to be

14
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.825
similar because these
are the same person.

15
00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.925
Whereas given this
pair of images,

16
00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:38.360
you want their
encodings to be quite

17
00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.555
different because these
are different persons.

18
00:00:41.555 --> 00:00:44.570
In the terminology
of the triplet loss,

19
00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.340
what you're going
to do is always

20
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.740
look at one anchor image,

21
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.260
and then you want
to distance between

22
00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.805
the anchor and a positive image,

23
00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:55.205
really a positive example,

24
00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:57.950
meaning is the same
person, to be similar.

25
00:00:57.950 --> 00:00:59.840
Whereas you want the anchor

26
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.550
when pairs are compared to have

27
00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:03.905
a negative example for

28
00:01:03.905 --> 00:01:07.360
their distances to be
much further apart.

29
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.790
This is what gives rise
to the term triplet loss,

30
00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:12.440
which is that you
always be looking

31
00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.850
at three images at a time.

32
00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.420
You'll be looking
at an anchor image,

33
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.160
a positive image, as well
as a negative image.

34
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.365
I'm going to abbreviate
anchor, positive,

35
00:01:24.365 --> 00:01:26.760
and negative as A, P,

36
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.540
and N. To formalize this,

37
00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.690
what you want is for
the parameters of

38
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:34.370
your neural network
or for your encoding

39
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.064
to have the following property;

40
00:01:36.064 --> 00:01:39.755
which is that you
want the encoding

41
00:01:39.755 --> 00:01:41.990
between the anchor minus

42
00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:44.450
the encoding of the
positive example,

43
00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.880
you want this to be
small, and in particular,

44
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.030
you want this to be
less than or equal to

45
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:52.985
the distance or the squared norm

46
00:01:52.985 --> 00:01:54.440
between the encoding of

47
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.110
the anchor and the
encoding of the negative,

48
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:02.490
whereof course this is d of A,

49
00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.765
P and this is d of A,

50
00:02:06.765 --> 00:02:11.300
N. You can think of d
as a distance function,

51
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.705
which is why we named it
with the alphabet d. Now,

52
00:02:14.705 --> 00:02:16.430
if we move the term from

53
00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:18.860
the right side of this
equation to the left side,

54
00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.185
what you end up with is f of

55
00:02:21.185 --> 00:02:25.295
A minus f of P squared minus,

56
00:02:25.295 --> 00:02:27.650
I'm going to take the
right-hand side now,

57
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:31.425
minus f of N squared,

58
00:02:31.425 --> 00:02:34.660
you want it to be less
than or equal to zero.

59
00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.040
But now we're going to
make a slight change

60
00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.940
to this expression, which is;

61
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:41.464
one trivial way to make
sure this is satisfied

62
00:02:41.464 --> 00:02:44.250
is to just learn
everything equals zero.

63
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.285
If f always output zero,

64
00:02:46.285 --> 00:02:48.740
then this is 0 minus
0, which is 0,

65
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:51.650
this is 0 minus 0, which
is 0, and so, well,

66
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:57.180
by saying f of any image
equals a vector of all zero's,

67
00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:01.450
you can see almost trivially
satisfy this equation.

68
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.740
To make sure that
the neural network

69
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.930
doesn't just output zero,

70
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.450
for all the encodings,

71
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.470
or to make sure
that it doesn't set

72
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.280
all the encodings
equal to each other.

73
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.000
Another way for
the neural network

74
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:16.160
to give a trivial outputs is

75
00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.020
if the encoding for
every image was

76
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:20.240
identical to the encoding
to every other image,

77
00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:25.215
in which case you
again get 0 minus 0.

78
00:03:25.215 --> 00:03:28.070
To prevent your neural
network from doing that,

79
00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:31.850
what we're going to do is
modify this objective to say

80
00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:33.470
that this doesn't need to be

81
00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.750
just less than equal to zero,

82
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:40.745
it needs to be quite a
bit smaller than zero.

83
00:03:40.745 --> 00:03:43.040
In particular, if we
say this needs to

84
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.810
be less than negative Alpha,

85
00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.265
where Alpha is another
hyperparameter

86
00:03:49.265 --> 00:03:51.230
then this prevents
a neural network

87
00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.600
from outputting the
trivial solutions.

88
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.955
By convention, usually, we write

89
00:03:55.955 --> 00:03:59.425
plus Alpha instead of
negative Alpha there.

90
00:03:59.425 --> 00:04:02.810
This is also called a margin,

91
00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:06.080
which is terminology that
you'd be familiar with

92
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.300
if you've also seen
the literature

93
00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.340
on support vector machines,

94
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:12.640
but don't worry about
it if you haven't.

95
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.185
We can also modify
this equation on top

96
00:04:15.185 --> 00:04:18.205
by adding this margin parameter.

97
00:04:18.205 --> 00:04:23.120
Given example, let's say
the margin is set to 0.2.

98
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.550
If in this example d of

99
00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:29.540
the anchor and the
positive is equal to 0.5,

100
00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.610
then you won't be satisfied if

101
00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.010
d between the anchor
and the negative,

102
00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:36.570
was just a little bit
bigger, say 0.51.

103
00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:41.600
Of one. Even though 0.51
is bigger than 0.5,

104
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.190
you're saying that's
not good enough.

105
00:04:43.190 --> 00:04:48.345
We want d of A,N to be
much bigger than d of A,P.

106
00:04:48.345 --> 00:04:50.700
In particular, you
want this to be

107
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.410
at least 0.7 or higher.

108
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.775
Alternatively,

109
00:04:54.775 --> 00:04:58.750
to achieve this margin or
this gap of at least 0.2,

110
00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:02.040
you could either push
this up or push this down

111
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:05.465
so that there is at
least this gap of

112
00:05:05.465 --> 00:05:09.365
this hyperparameter
Alpha 0.2 between

113
00:05:09.365 --> 00:05:11.450
the distance between
the anchor and the

114
00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.580
positive versus the
anchor and the negative.

115
00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.570
That's what having a margin
parameter here does.

116
00:05:17.570 --> 00:05:18.830
Which is it pushes

117
00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:21.900
the anchor-positive pair and

118
00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:25.645
the anchor-negative pair
further away from each other.

119
00:05:25.645 --> 00:05:28.570
Let's take this equation
we have here at

120
00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:31.195
the bottom and on
the next slide,

121
00:05:31.195 --> 00:05:35.620
formalize it and define
the triplet loss function.

122
00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.870
The triplet loss function is

123
00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.865
defined on triples of images.

124
00:05:40.865 --> 00:05:44.835
Given three images: A,

125
00:05:44.835 --> 00:05:48.969
P, and N, the anchor positive
and negative examples,

126
00:05:48.969 --> 00:05:51.030
so the positive examples is of

127
00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.245
the same person as the anchor,

128
00:05:53.245 --> 00:05:54.600
but the negative is of

129
00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.605
a different person
than the anchor.

130
00:05:57.605 --> 00:06:01.530
We're going to define
the loss as follows.

131
00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.084
The loss on this example,

132
00:06:03.084 --> 00:06:06.390
which is really defined on
a triplet of images is,

133
00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:10.085
let me first copy over what
we had on the previous slide.

134
00:06:10.085 --> 00:06:16.060
That was f A minus f P squared

135
00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:23.674
minus f A minus f N squared,

136
00:06:23.674 --> 00:06:26.845
and then plus alpha,
the margin parameter.

137
00:06:26.845 --> 00:06:28.870
What you want is for this to

138
00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.075
be less than or equal to zero.

139
00:06:32.075 --> 00:06:34.390
To define the loss function,

140
00:06:34.390 --> 00:06:39.265
let's take the max
between this and zero.

141
00:06:39.265 --> 00:06:42.040
The effect of taking
the max here is that

142
00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.229
so long as this is
less than zero,

143
00:06:45.229 --> 00:06:47.095
then the loss is zero

144
00:06:47.095 --> 00:06:48.965
because the max is
something less than equal

145
00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:52.790
to zero with zero is
going to be zero.

146
00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.510
So long as you
achieve the goal of

147
00:06:54.510 --> 00:06:56.760
making this thing I've
underlined in green,

148
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.475
so long as you've
achieved the objective of

149
00:06:58.475 --> 00:07:00.980
making that less than
or equal to zero,

150
00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:04.045
then the loss on this
example is equal to zero.

151
00:07:04.045 --> 00:07:05.385
But if on the other hand,

152
00:07:05.385 --> 00:07:07.654
if this is greater than zero,

153
00:07:07.654 --> 00:07:09.130
then if you take the max,

154
00:07:09.130 --> 00:07:10.674
the max will end up selecting

155
00:07:10.674 --> 00:07:11.800
this thing I've underlined in

156
00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:15.425
green and so you'd
have a positive loss.

157
00:07:15.425 --> 00:07:17.470
By trying to minimize this,

158
00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.805
this has the effect
of trying to send

159
00:07:19.805 --> 00:07:23.490
this thing to be zero or
less than equal to zero.

160
00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.550
Then so long as this zero
or less than equal to zero,

161
00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:28.715
the neural network doesn't care

162
00:07:28.715 --> 00:07:31.940
how much further negative it is.

163
00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:33.995
This is how you
define the loss on

164
00:07:33.995 --> 00:07:36.220
a single triplet and the

165
00:07:36.220 --> 00:07:38.980
overall cost function for
your neural network can

166
00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:42.580
be sum over a training set of

167
00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:47.530
these individual losses
on different triplets.

168
00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:51.790
If you have a
training set of say,

169
00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:55.090
10,000 pictures with
1,000 different persons,

170
00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.605
what you'd have to
do is take your

171
00:07:57.605 --> 00:08:00.425
10,000 pictures and
use it to generate,

172
00:08:00.425 --> 00:08:02.825
to select triplets like this,

173
00:08:02.825 --> 00:08:05.320
and then train your
learning algorithm using

174
00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:08.045
gradient descent on this
type of cost function,

175
00:08:08.045 --> 00:08:09.910
which is really
defined on triplets

176
00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:13.630
of images drawn from
your training set.

177
00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:19.659
Notice that in order to define
this dataset of triplets,

178
00:08:19.659 --> 00:08:22.895
you do need some
pairs of A and P,

179
00:08:22.895 --> 00:08:25.340
pairs of pictures
of the same person.

180
00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.520
For the purpose of
training your system,

181
00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.280
you do need a dataset where you

182
00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.240
have multiple pictures
of the same person.

183
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:34.895
That's why in this example
I said if you have

184
00:08:34.895 --> 00:08:38.140
10,000 pictures of 1,000
different persons,

185
00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:40.160
so maybe you have ten pictures,

186
00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:41.775
on average of each of your

187
00:08:41.775 --> 00:08:44.315
1,000 persons to make
up your entire dataset.

188
00:08:44.315 --> 00:08:47.140
If you had just one
picture of each person,

189
00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.625
then you can't actually
train this system.

190
00:08:49.625 --> 00:08:56.570
But of course, after
having trained a system,

191
00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:58.210
you can then apply it to

192
00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.950
your one-shot learning problem

193
00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:02.710
where for your face
recognition system,

194
00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:04.935
maybe you have only
a single picture

195
00:09:04.935 --> 00:09:06.990
of someone you might be
trying to recognize.

196
00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:08.330
But for your training set,

197
00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:09.935
you do need to
make sure you have

198
00:09:09.935 --> 00:09:12.340
multiple images of
the same person,

199
00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.245
at least for some people
in your training set,

200
00:09:14.245 --> 00:09:18.725
so that you can have pairs of
anchor and positive images.

201
00:09:18.725 --> 00:09:21.840
Now, how do you actually

202
00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:24.955
choose these triplets to
form your training set?

203
00:09:24.955 --> 00:09:28.330
One of the problems
is if you choose A,

204
00:09:28.330 --> 00:09:31.175
P, and N randomly from
your training set,

205
00:09:31.175 --> 00:09:33.285
subject to A and P being

206
00:09:33.285 --> 00:09:36.110
the same person and A and
N being different persons,

207
00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:38.105
one of the problems is that if

208
00:09:38.105 --> 00:09:40.160
you choose them so
that they're random,

209
00:09:40.160 --> 00:09:44.175
then this constraint is
very easy to satisfy.

210
00:09:44.175 --> 00:09:48.409
Because given two randomly
chosen pictures of people,

211
00:09:48.409 --> 00:09:53.170
chances are A and N are much
different than A and P.

212
00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:55.880
I hope you still
recognize this notation.

213
00:09:55.880 --> 00:09:59.150
Does d (A, P) will be high

214
00:09:59.150 --> 00:10:02.825
written on the last few
slides of these encoding.

215
00:10:02.825 --> 00:10:05.120
This is equal to

216
00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.060
this squared norm
distance between

217
00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:10.910
the encodings that we had
on the previous line.

218
00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:14.660
But if A and N are two randomly
chosen different persons,

219
00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.310
then there's a very
high chance that

220
00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:18.080
this will be much bigger,

221
00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:20.060
more than the margin helper,

222
00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.920
than that term on the left and

223
00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.710
the Neural Network won't
learn much from it.

224
00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:26.000
To construct your training set,

225
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:28.460
what you want to do is
to choose triplets, A,

226
00:10:28.460 --> 00:10:31.265
P, and N, they're the
''hard'' to train on.

227
00:10:31.265 --> 00:10:34.430
In particular, what you
want is for all triplets

228
00:10:34.430 --> 00:10:38.850
that this constraint
be satisfied.

229
00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:42.320
A triplet that is
''hard'' would be if you

230
00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.930
choose values for A, P,

231
00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:47.570
and N so that may be d (A,

232
00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:52.565
P) is actually quite
close to d (A, N).

233
00:10:52.565 --> 00:10:55.190
In that case, the
learning algorithm has

234
00:10:55.190 --> 00:10:58.190
to try extra hard to take
this thing on the right

235
00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:00.290
and try to push it up or take

236
00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:02.885
this thing on the left
and try to push it down

237
00:11:02.885 --> 00:11:05.570
so that there is at
least a margin of

238
00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.570
alpha between the left
side and the right side.

239
00:11:08.570 --> 00:11:11.885
The effect of choosing
these triplets is that it

240
00:11:11.885 --> 00:11:14.750
increases the
computational efficiency

241
00:11:14.750 --> 00:11:16.340
of your learning algorithm.

242
00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:18.500
If you choose the
triplets randomly,

243
00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:20.150
then too many triplets would be

244
00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.565
really easy and gradient descent

245
00:11:22.565 --> 00:11:24.665
won't do anything because
you're Neural Network would

246
00:11:24.665 --> 00:11:27.110
get them right pretty
much all the time.

247
00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:29.510
It's only by choosing
''hard'' to triplets

248
00:11:29.510 --> 00:11:32.254
that the gradient
descent procedure

249
00:11:32.254 --> 00:11:34.925
has to do some work
to try to push

250
00:11:34.925 --> 00:11:38.765
these quantities further
away from those quantities.

251
00:11:38.765 --> 00:11:41.690
If you're interested,
the details are

252
00:11:41.690 --> 00:11:45.530
presented in this paper
by Florian Schroff,

253
00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.845
Dmitry Kalenichenko,
and James Philbin,

254
00:11:48.845 --> 00:11:51.305
where they have a
system called FaceNet,

255
00:11:51.305 --> 00:11:53.030
which is where a lot
of the ideas I'm

256
00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:55.610
presenting in this
video had come from.

257
